LAST_SUCCESS=Task 117 M2 — VAAPI H.264 decode CAPABILITY confirmed on fedora (Fedora 43, Intel HD 4000, /dev/dri/renderD128 world-accessible RW, i965+iHD drivers, glibc 2.42, rustc 1.92). Proof: oxideav registered vaapi-h264@10, which it only does when vaQueryConfigEntrypoints finds VAEntrypointVLD for H.264 (it SKIPPED on WSL where the profile is absent). So libva init + H264 VLD decode profile + max-dims query all succeeded on the HW.
NOTES=oxideav's vaapi decode = 0/300 (software --no-hwaccel = 300/300) — SAME systemic silent-HW-fail as nvdec (popos) and vulkan (WSL): all 3 oxideav HW backends broken, never fall back. NOT a hardware limit (capability is advertised). CONCLUSION: hardware ready, but we must write our OWN VAAPI backend (oxideav unusable for HW); fallback must key on frames-produced (the wandr-video registry contract already does). popos is DOWN (no route); use fedora (ssh harry@fedora, render node world-RW, no sudo). No vainfo, no passwordless sudo on fedora, but libva 2.22 + i965 driver + libva-intel-media-driver installed. NEXT: write crates/wandr-video/src/backends/vaapi.rs — VADisplay via drm render node, VAProfileH264*/VAEntrypointVLD, slice-param decode, register at priority ~10, kind=Hardware, MUST self-verify (decode a probe keyframe) at open per the registry contract. Test with --video-decode-file on fedora.
